DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Plekhanov's general philosophy centers on "monism" — the claim that a
single, coherent materialist-dialectical worldview can dissolve the
dualisms (spirit/matter, freedom/necessity, subject/object) that he
took to fragment prior philosophy and historiography. His 1895
treatise, translated as "The Development of the Monist View of
History" (also published as "In Defence of Materialism"), is his
programmatic statement: it constructs a lineage running from the
eighteenth-century French materialists (Holbach, Helvétius) and the
utopian socialists, through Hegel's dialectic and Feuerbach's
anthropological materialism, to Marx and Engels, whom Plekhanov
presents as synthesizing and completing this tradition. Plekhanov is
generally credited with coining the Russian term corresponding to
"dialectical materialism" and with giving Marxism its first
systematic philosophical (rather than purely economic or political)
exposition, in works such as "Essays in the History of Materialism"
(a historical-critical study of Holbach and Helvétius) and the later
"Fundamental Problems of Marxism."

The most philosophically contested piece of Plekhanov's system is his
epistemology, especially his adoption (via Helmholtz) of a
"hieroglyph" or "symbol" theory of sensation — the view that
sensations are not literal copies ("images") of external objects but
conventional signs or "hieroglyphs" that nonetheless correlate
reliably with an external, mind-independent reality. This move let
Plekhanov combine a strong realist/materialist ontology with an
admission that perception is mediated and non-transparent, but it drew
a documented objection from Engels (in correspondence) that treating
sensations as mere "hieroglyphs" rather than "images" risked
reintroducing Kantian agnosticism about the thing-in-itself — a
worry later pressed, from opposite directions, by Russian
empiriocritics (Bogdanov and allies) and by Lenin. The literature
recovered here (Steila's monograph on the genesis of Plekhanov's
epistemology, known through Wagner's review; Rybas and Rumyantseva on
Plekhanov's use and abuse of Kant's "thing-in-itself"; Sériot on the
longer Soviet afterlife of "hieroglyph" polemics) reconstructs why
this doctrine became such a persistent flashpoint. A second major
strand concerns Plekhanov's monism as applied to Spinoza: Steila,
Steinberg, and Soboleva show that Plekhanov read Spinoza's substance
monism as an early, theologically-clothed anticipation of dialectical
materialism, a reading Bogdanov attacked on both hermeneutic and
systematic grounds, and Deborin and Akselrod later modified. Finally,
van Ree, White, Moore, and Jordan variously test how faithful
Plekhanov's construction of the Hegel-Feuerbach-Marx-Engels lineage
actually is to the source texts, generally finding him a
systematizer and occasional reviser rather than a strict transmitter
of Marx's own, less settled, dialectical materialism.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the philosophical bedrock — monism, dialectics,
epistemology, and the constructed materialist tradition — that
underwrites Plekhanov's better-known theory of history. A literature
review on Plekhanov cannot assess his philosophy of history (treated
in the companion domain) without first establishing what his monism
and hieroglyph epistemology actually claim and how contested they
were among contemporaries and later scholars.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Secondary literature specifically on Plekhanov's engagement with the
utopian socialists (Saint-Simon, Fourier) proved very thin; searches
returned general histories of utopian socialism but nothing
substantively linking them to Plekhanov's own texts. Coverage of the
1993 Wagner review is used as a proxy for Steila's monograph itself,
since the book (Sovietica series, c. 1991) carries no independently
verifiable DOI/CrossRef record; readers should consult the monograph
directly where possible. Seven entries are marked INCOMPLETE (no
abstract resolvable from Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, or NDPR)
and are excluded from synthesis: the three Plekhanov primary texts
(1895/1947, 1896/1934, 1969 — all pre-dating digital abstract
indexing), Jordan's 1967 book, Yassour's 1983 article, Wagner's 1993
review, and Hu's forthcoming 2026 article. Their CORE ARGUMENT/
RELEVANCE annotations above are grounded in search snippets, review
excerpts, and (for the primary texts) their own well-documented
publication contexts rather than in indexed abstracts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around three axes: (1) monism as method (the
Hegel–Feuerbach–French-materialist–Marx lineage Plekhanov constructs);
(2) the hieroglyph/reflection theory of knowledge and the
Engels/Lenin/Bogdanov objections to it; (3) fidelity debates — how
much of "dialectical materialism" is Plekhanov's invention versus a
transmission of Marx's and Engels's own (less systematic) views.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Plekhanov's monism/dialectical materialism as synthesis: 3 papers
  (primary texts) - his own programmatic statements.
- Hieroglyph/symbol theory of knowledge and its critics: 5 papers -
  Wagner/Steila, Rybas, Rumyantseva, Sériot, Pavlov.
- Monism via Spinoza and internal Marxist rebuttals: 3 papers -
  Steila, Steinberg, Soboleva.
- Fidelity to Marx/Engels/Hegel: 4 papers - van Ree, White, Moore,
  Jordan.
- Recent reassessment of Plekhanov's reading of Marx: 2 papers -
  Hu, Yassour.
====================================================================
}

